module v2.compiler.build_workspace_grant


data build_workspace_grant_note: String = "FLAG A interim (docs/plans/effect-namespace-grants.md 6, owned silent-ibex-417): the FIRST envelope row, dissolving the undecidable 'can a build run hermetically?' question into a SHAPE over the landed std.effect_grant model -- a row, not a mode exception. A build is admissible in a replay-lane run when its envelope is: Read under the substrate closure, the pinned toolchain root, AND its own workspace subtree; Write under its own workspace subtree; and NO network grant. The workspace Read grant resolves the ownership-implies-read question the initial row deferred (P-B, build_transport_admission): a real build reads its own workspace -- the emitted sources it was handed and the intermediate artifacts it produces during linking -- so the workspace is Read+Write, not Write alone. The 'no network' property is fail-closed BY CONSTRUCTION (DESIGN 5): the absence of a UriTree grant means admit_effect refuses any network target -- there is no arm to widen. This is the SINGLE build-envelope authority (DESIGN 3); its one consumer is v2.compiler.build_transport_admission.build_transport_admissible. dissolve-on = P-B wires std.effect_grant.admit_effect into the v2 effect-io host build/write dispatch -- src/v2/extdeps/runtimes/v2_effect_io_host.effect_io_host_dispatch_write blanket-refuses every hermetic write today (effect_io_host_hermetic_write_rejected, gated on the boolean execution_mode_is_wet_dispatch); that gate becomes an admit_effect(env, Write, target) check whose refusal is the modeled EffectOutsideGrant, and the emit-host build transport (src/v2/compiler/emit_host.emit_host_run_transport, a v1_interpreter.rs intrinsic) runs only on an Admitted verdict."

data build_workspace_grant_lifecycle_note: String = "Lifecycle grades, carried honestly (std.effect_grant.LifecycleGrade, the construction/validation axis of DESIGN 5). The substrate-closure and pinned-toolchain Read grants are LifecycleByConstruction: content-addressed / pinned inputs are immutable, so no persistence-past-frame concern applies. The workspace Read and Write grants are the honest weaker LifecycleByConvention: an interim /tmp-style scratch whose cleanup can fail or be forgotten (the same subtree the build reads its own intermediates from). Upgrading it to LifecycleByConstruction (ephemeral container / netns-scoped scratch that teardown erases) is a later row, never conflated with the strong grade now. HANDOFF (P3-native lane): the pinned toolchain Read root is modeled here as a POSITION; its version must be folded into the build artifact key (content-addressing) so the build's inputs are replayable -- keyed there, positioned here."

fn build_workspace_grant_envelope(
  workspace_root: NamespacePosition,
  substrate_closure_root: NamespacePosition,
  toolchain_root: NamespacePosition,
  frame: Frame
) -> Envelope {
  Envelope {
    frame: frame,
    grants: [
      Grant {
        verb: Read,
        root: substrate_closure_root,
        binding: RealTransport,
        lifecycle: LifecycleByConstruction
      },
      Grant {
        verb: Read,
        root: toolchain_root,
        binding: RealTransport,
        lifecycle: LifecycleByConstruction
      },
      Grant {
        verb: Read,
        root: workspace_root,
        binding: RealTransport,
        lifecycle: LifecycleByConvention
      },
      Grant {
        verb: Write,
        root: workspace_root,
        binding: RealTransport,
        lifecycle: LifecycleByConvention
      },
    ]
  }
}
